# The Phase 1 fidelity subject: run `macchina`, a real system-info TUI that
# exercises coloured labels, a vertical bar, Unicode glyphs and a busy grid of
# short text runs — the kind of output a recording tool is actually judged on,
# as opposed to a bare shell prompt. `scripts/inspect/compare.sh` burns this
# tape and burns `macchina.vhs.tape` (the closest VHS can get to the same
# terminal) and diffs the two.
#
# Every `Set` below has a value chosen to match the VHS twin as closely as the
# two languages allow. Anything without a `dvd` equivalent is noted there, not
# here, since this is the file that has everything `dvd` supports.

Set Shell "bash"
Set FontFamily "Menlo"
Set FontSize 18
Set Width 1200
Set Height 700
Set Padding 24
Set Theme "dracula"
Set Framerate 30
Set TypingSpeed 40ms
# Not `false`. `Set CursorBlink false` (also the burn-time default) makes
# crates/dvd/src/burn.rs:374 write the raw byte sequence `\x1b[?12l` through
# `session.write`, which delivers it to the PTY as if a user had typed it,
# rather than feeding it to the VT parser that models the emulated terminal's
# own state. The shell's line editor cannot interpret a DECRST-12 control
# sequence arriving as keyboard input; it re-echoes fragments of it and
# spliced them into the very next keystrokes, and on this machine that turns
# the first `Type "macchina"` below into a bogus, failing command (confirmed
# by burning this tape with the setting flipped both ways — see the W7
# report). `true` skips that write entirely, at the cost of a cursor that can
# be mid-blink in whichever frame `ffmpeg` happens to land on.
Set CursorBlink true

# A beat before the first keystroke, so `Type` does not race the shell's own
# startup banner — without it, characters typed before the shell has claimed
# the terminal can interleave with its boot output instead of landing after
# the prompt.
Sleep 500ms

Type "macchina"
Enter

# `Wait` blocks on the shell prompt reappearing, i.e. until macchina has
# finished printing and control has returned — the same signal a human would
# wait for before taking a screenshot.
Wait

# macchina's own output settles the instant it prints, but real terminals and
# `vhs`'s ttyd+chromium capture path both need a beat to stop redrawing; this
# keeps the still comparable to VHS's without becoming the thing being timed.
Sleep 2s
